# Legal pages — /terms and /privacy are served natively by Cloudflare Pages from
# terms.html / privacy.html, exactly like /about and /projects.
#
# DO NOT re-add `/terms /terms.html 200`-style rewrites. A 200-rewrite whose target
# ends in `.html` fights Pages' built-in `.html` -> clean-URL 308 and the route loops
# forever:
#   /terms -> 308 /terms -> 308 /terms -> ...   (curl: Maximum (50) redirects followed)
# It was live on supercompute.io: both legal pages were unreachable in production
# (2026-09-11), which is what the TikTok Content Posting app form needs to point at.
# Control that isolates the cause: /about.html -> 308 /about -> 200 (no rule, works)
# and /terms.html -> 308 /terms -> loop (rule present). Same export, same normalisation.
# The rules were added 2026-07-29 (b6e80dd) because the pages did not exist then, so the
# `/*  /index.html  200` catch-all below served index.html for /terms. pages/terms.tsx
# and pages/privacy.tsx exist again since PR #18 — with real .html assets the catch-all
# no longer applies and the rewrites are both unnecessary and harmful.

# NewsDesk article routing:
# Markdown articles (aave, aerodrome, nypd, newsdesk-launch, token-gating,
# on-chain-identity, welcome-to-supercompute) get their own pre-rendered HTML
# files at /newsdesk/<slug>.html — Cloudflare Pages serves these directly.
#
# D1-only articles (no markdown source) fall through to the placeholder shell
# at /newsdesk/article.html, which fetches content from /api/articles client-side.
# List each D1-only slug explicitly. Must come BEFORE the /* SPA catch-all.
/newsdesk/the-network-nobody-wanted-you-to-see    /newsdesk/article   200
/newsdesk/decentralization-as-spectrum            /newsdesk/article   200
/newsdesk/platform-phase-1-launch                 /newsdesk/article   200
/newsdesk/ens-on-chain-identity                   /newsdesk/article   200
/newsdesk/defi-yield-strategies-2026              /newsdesk/article   200
/newsdesk/base-ecosystem-guide                    /newsdesk/article   200
/newsdesk/refi-deep-dive                          /newsdesk/article   200

# Dynamic article templates — map any slug to the [id] shell (client-side fetch).
# Must come BEFORE the /* SPA catch-all.
/app/article/regular/*    /app/article/regular/[id]    200
/app/article/kg/*         /app/article/kg/[id]         200
/app/publishing/*/edit    /app/publishing/[id]/edit    200

# Marketplace listing detail — /project/[id] is a statically exported shell that reads the
# listing id from the client router and fetches /api/marketplace?id=<id>. Because the site
# uses `output: "export"`, only the literal /project/[id] asset exists, so a real id
# (/project/abc123) must be mapped onto that shell here. Without this rule every
# "View listing" link rendered by /marketplace 404s on production.
# Must come BEFORE the /* SPA catch-all.
/project/*    /project/[id]    200

# NO CATCH-ALL SPA FALLBACK HERE.
# This site is a Next.js static export (`output: "export"` in next.config.js), so every
# route has a real file in out/ — /terms -> terms.html, /staking -> staking.html,
# /app/projects/new -> app/projects/new.html — and Cloudflare Pages maps clean URLs onto
# those files itself.
#
# A `/*  /index.html  200` SPA fallback was rejected by the Pages redirects parser as an
# infinite loop (the /index.html target is matched by /* again), so it was silently ignored
# both locally and in production. Verified independently 2026-09-16:
#   /definitely-not-a-real-page-xyz -> HTTP 404, the exported 404 shell (2,585 B)
#   /                               -> HTTP 200, index.html (7,956 B)
# i.e. unknown paths fall through to 404.html, NOT to index.html. Removing the dead rule
# changes no live behaviour: real routes keep serving their own file.
#
# NOTE: the "Must come BEFORE the /* SPA catch-all" annotations above are now historical —
# the catch-all they referred to does not exist. The specific rules above still matter
# relative to ONE ANOTHER, so they stay listed in this order.
